Ladybird’s sidePanel namespace requires the sidePanel permission and
its sidebarAction namespace the manifest’s sidebar_action key; both
drive one panel per window. sidePanel.open() needs a user activation
and rejects with the fixed string otherwise, as do sidebarAction’s
open(), close(), and toggle(); every other refusal uses a fixed string
too, and a per-tab page set through sidebarAction is dropped when the
tab navigates while one set through sidePanel stays.

Chrome’s side_panel.idl gates the namespace on the sidePanel permission,
and SidePanelOpenFunction::Run() refuses without user_gesture() with
“`sidePanel.open()` may only be called in response to a user gesture.”,
then with “At least one of `tabId` and `windowId` must be provided”;
SidePanelService::OpenSidePanelForWindow() and OpenSidePanelForTab()
answer “No active side panel for windowId: N” and “... tabId: N” when
the options have no path or are disabled. Its per-tab options outlive
navigations, and SetOpenSidePanelOnIconClick() makes the action icon
open the panel instead of the popup.

Firefox’s sidebar_action.json gives sidebarAction to every extension
that has the manifest key, marks open(), close(), and toggle() as
requireUserInput, and ext-sidebarAction.js refuses tabId and windowId
together (“Only one of tabId and windowId can be specified.”), keeps a
panel, title, and icon per tab and per window, and drops a tab’s when
the tab navigates somewhere else.

WebKit’s WebExtensionContextAPISidebarCocoa.mm implements both
namespaces over one WebExtensionSidebar model, refuses both identifiers
at once, and refuses open() for a disabled sidebar or one without a
panel; it has no gesture check of its own.
